# Candidate-only differences, one case name followed by the reviewed reason.
tas_write BlastEm's TAS memory path did not set bit 7 or emit the write in the pinned synthetic fixture; nmd TAS is covered by bus_check and the Motorola byte read-modify-write contract.
dma_memory_vram Pinned BlastEm emits H32 VRAM source words every 20 master clocks, while Sega's Technical Overview gives 167 transfer bytes per VBlank line; nmd models the manual's two-byte source word cadence.
dma_active_vram_h32 BlastEm emits H32 DMA source words every 20 master clocks with DISP enabled on active line 21; Sega's Technical Overview gives 16 transfer bytes per effective line, so nmd uses the manual-derived slower active rate.
dma_active_vram_h40 BlastEm emits H40 DMA source words every 16 master clocks with DISP enabled on active line 21; Sega's Technical Overview gives 18 transfer bytes per effective line, so nmd uses the manual-derived slower active rate.
sound_psg_data_only_volume BlastEm ignores a data-only write when the PSG volume register is selected; the TI SN76489AN datasheet says subsequent data continues into the latched register, so nmd applies 0x3F as channel 0 mute (https://map.grauw.nl/resources/sound/texas_instruments_sn76489an.pdf).
sound_psg_tone3_noise_switch BlastEm copies tone 3's divisor into a separate noise counter when selecting the tone-3 source; the YM7101 die reconstruction clocks from tone 3's rising edge, so nmd uses the actual edge (https://github.com/nukeykt/Nuked-MD/blob/main/vdp.c#L5908-L5919).
